{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 41, :comments ["<b>Bamoth-baal.</b> In three places he built twenty-one altars: seven at Bamoth-baal, and seven in the field of Zophim at the top of the peak, and seven at Baal-peor — totalling twenty-one. And he would offer upon each altar a bull and a ram, so it is found that all his offerings in these three places were forty-two offerings. And as for what it mentions 'cattle and sheep,' Rashi, of blessed memory, explained it as a small matter." "<b>And one may wonder</b> what the master saw to explain thus, for 'cattle' is a general term. And it is possible that he needed to explain thus because Balaam did this intentionally, so as not to prolong the meal and curse Israel; but there is no restriction (me'ut) in the implication of the language." "<b>And from there he saw the edge of the people.</b> And not all of them, because they were encamped in the four directions of the heavens. And Balak's intention each time was to bring Balaam up to a high place, so that he might see the people from there and direct his gaze toward them by the power of looking — for this is among the forces of the soul, as in the matter mentioned in the words of the Sages, of blessed memory: the Rav said to the wine-pourer, 'I wish to show him; let him fix his eyes upon him and his soul will rest.' And the reason for this matter that was customary among the Sages, of blessed memory, was because of the weakening of the mind of the perfect sage, for whom it is impossible to be without thought of Torah even for a moment; and since they weaken his mind and thought, the wisdom he is constantly engaged in is interrupted — and whoever causes this is like one who cuts down saplings, and the curse is fitting to fall upon him. And of this Scripture says (Ecclesiastes 10:9): 'He who hews stones shall be wounded by them' — for one who causes the interruption and cleaving of wisdom, which is a tree of life, is fitting to be endangered himself. Even though we find among the supreme pious ones, great in wisdom, those for whom speech and weakening of mind make no impression — for the matter is an innate disposition (segullah) in the soul; and besides the wisdom of a person, this innate disposition must also be present in his intellective soul. And when these two things are combined in him — that wisdom is planted in him and this innate disposition is in his soul — then he will perform wonders upon whoever weakens his mind, for that person is burned by his own ember."], :book :numbers, :voice :rabbeinu-bahya, :lang :en, :chapter 22, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T14:35:21.002992412Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}